2025-05-01 03:13:28 - Thread-1242 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant tables / columns and how they will be used  

• olist_orders  
  – order_id : join key to payments table  
  – customer_id : join key to customers table  
  – order_status : filter to keep only 'delivered' records  

• olist_order_payments  
  – order_id : join key back to orders table  
  – payment_value : value to be summed per city  

• olist_customers  
  – customer_id : join key back to orders table  
  – customer_city : city to be grouped / ranked  

Typical condition(s) to apply  
• olist_orders.order_status = 'delivered'  

Below are ten exploratory → final SQL queries (SQLite dialect).  
Every query returns at most 20 rows and is wrapped with a short comment.

```sql
-- 1) Distinct order status values to see what exists
SELECT DISTINCT "order_status"
FROM "olist_orders"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Sample list of cities appearing in customers table
SELECT DISTINCT "customer_city"
FROM "olist_customers"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Example of delivered orders and their linked customer_id
SELECT "order_id",
       "customer_id",
       "order_status"
FROM   "olist_orders"
WHERE  "order_status" = 'delivered'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Raw payment records (one row per payment event)
SELECT "order_id",
       "payment_value"
FROM   "olist_order_payments"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Orders that have more than one payment record (sanity‑check)
SELECT "order_id",
       COUNT(*) AS payment_records
FROM   "olist_order_payments"
GROUP  BY "order_id"
HAVING COUNT(*) > 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Join orders ↔ customers to fetch cities of delivered orders
SELECT o."order_id",
       c."customer_city"
FROM   "olist_orders"   AS o
JOIN   "olist_customers" AS c
       ON o."customer_id" = c."customer_id"
WHERE  o."order_status" = 'delivered'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Orders + payments + cities in one view (delivered only)
SELECT o."order_id",
       c."customer_city",
       p."payment_value"
FROM   "olist_orders"         AS o
JOIN   "olist_order_payments" AS p
       ON o."order_id" = p."order_id"
JOIN   "olist_customers"      AS c
       ON o."customer_id" = c."customer_id"
WHERE  o."order_status" = 'delivered'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Aggregate totals per city (delivered only); lowest first
SELECT c."customer_city",
       SUM(p."payment_value")              AS total_payments,
       COUNT(DISTINCT o."order_id")        AS delivered_orders
FROM   "olist_orders"         AS o
JOIN   "olist_order_payments" AS p
       ON o."order_id" = p."order_id"
JOIN   "olist_customers"      AS c
       ON o."customer_id" = c."customer_id"
WHERE  o."order_status" = 'delivered'
GROUP  BY c."customer_city"
ORDER  BY total_payments ASC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Five cities with the lowest summed payments
SELECT city_stats."customer_city",
       city_stats.total_payments,
       city_stats.delivered_orders
FROM (
        SELECT c."customer_city",
               SUM(p."payment_value")       AS total_payments,
               COUNT(DISTINCT o."order_id") AS delivered_orders
        FROM   "olist_orders"         AS o
        JOIN   "olist_order_payments" AS p
               ON o."order_id" = p."order_id"
        JOIN   "olist_customers"      AS c
               ON o."customer_id" = c."customer_id"
        WHERE  o."order_status" = 'delivered'
        GROUP  BY c."customer_city"
        ORDER  BY total_payments ASC
        LIMIT 5
     ) AS city_stats
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10) Final answer: averages of (a) total payments and (b) delivered‑order counts
--     over the 5 lowest‑payment cities
SELECT AVG(city_stats.total_payments)  AS avg_total_payments,
       AVG(city_stats.delivered_orders) AS avg_delivered_orders
FROM (
        SELECT c."customer_city",
               SUM(p."payment_value")       AS total_payments,
               COUNT(DISTINCT o."order_id") AS delivered_orders
        FROM   "olist_orders"         AS o
        JOIN   "olist_order_payments" AS p
               ON o."order_id" = p."order_id"
        JOIN   "olist_customers"      AS c
               ON o."customer_id" = c."customer_id"
        WHERE  o."order_status" = 'delivered'
        GROUP  BY c."customer_city"
        ORDER  BY total_payments ASC
        LIMIT 5
     ) AS city_stats
LIMIT 20;
```
